WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
Modern phishing beyond suspicious emails.

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.630
Sarah checks her inbox every morning.

3
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:05.650
She's careful.

4
00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.490
She hasn't clicked on a phishing email in years.

5
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.650
But today, phishing isn't coming only through her inbox.

6
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.770
Phishing used to be easy to spot.

7
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.030
Bad grammar, broken logos, a stranger's email address.

8
00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:18.030
Not anymore.

9
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.090
Attackers now use AI to write flawless messages,

10
00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.410
clone real websites, pixel for pixel,

11
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.170
and build fake pages in minutes.

12
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.510
The old warning signs are disappearing.

13
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.710
Two of today's most common attacks reached Sarah

14
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.270
in ways she never expected.

15
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.970
Here's how they reached Sarah.

16
00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.870
Part one, phishing, phishing through QR codes.

17
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.170
8:45 a.m., Sarah stops by her local cafe before work.

18
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.750
There's a poster near the counter,

19
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.950
scan to connect to the cafe's Wi-Fi.

20
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.010
She scans it.

21
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.470
The page that opens asks her to sign in

22
00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.710
with her work login to verify her device before connecting.

23
00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.210
Her thumb hovers over the sign-in button.

24
00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:55.070
She stops.

25
00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.770
The cafe didn't put up this QR code.

26
00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.210
Someone stuck a fake sticker over the real one.

27
00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.690
This is called quishing, phishing through QR codes.

28
00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.790
And this is where AI comes in,

29
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.290
building a fake login page used to take real skill and time.

30
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:13.710
Now, AI tools can clone an official sign-in page in seconds,

31
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.710
matching the real logo, layout,

32
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.630
and wording almost perfectly.

33
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.630
That's why Sarah can't tell the difference just by looking.

34
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.050
If she types her password, the attacker has it.

35
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.090
Sarah can't see where the QR code goes before she scans it.

36
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:29.510
That's the whole trick.

37
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.690
AI makes the destination look completely real.

38
00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:34.750
How to stay safe from quishing attacks.

39
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.250
Three simple habits.

40
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.250
One, when your phone previews a QR code link,

41
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:40.610
read it before you tap.

42
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.070
If it doesn't look like a website you recognize,

43
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:44.410
don't open it.

44
00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.110
Two, if a page asks you to log in after scanning a code,

45
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.210
be extra careful.

46
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.550
Free Wi-Fi and menus rarely need your work password.

47
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.730
Remember, a page can look perfect and still be fake.

48
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.170
If you spot a suspicious QR sticker in a public space,

49
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:00.190
don't scan it.

50
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.030
Report it to the venue or your workplace.

51
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.150
Part two, ClickFix.

52
00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.690
Phishing through fake verification steps.

53
00:02:07.169 --> 00:02:09.490
A week later, Sarah gets an email

54
00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.230
that looks like it's from a supplier she works with often.

55
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.550
A routine notice about an invoice she needs to review.

56
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.690
It's polite, professional, and expected.

57
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.130
She clicks the link.

58
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.350
A page loads that looks like an official security check.

59
00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.990
The page asks her to verify details to view the document.

60
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.910
The box says, verify you're human.

61
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.990
Follow these three keyboard steps.

62
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.150
It looks official, technical,

63
00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.730
like something a real website would ask.

64
00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:38.710
Sarah's fingers move toward the keyboard.

65
00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:39.570
Freezes.

66
00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:40.930
Cursor pulled back.

67
00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.510
This is called ClickFix.

68
00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.170
And it's important to know.

69
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.650
It reaches you through channels that already feel safe.

70
00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:48.870
A routine email.

71
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.150
A familiar website.

72
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:51.490
A link a colleague shared.

73
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.310
That's what gets people to click without thinking twice.

74
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.150
Here's what's actually happening.

75
00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.830
When Sarah clicked the link,

76
00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.950
the page secretly copied a hidden command onto her clipboard.

77
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.510
The same clipboard that stores anything she copies and pastes.

78
00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.270
The first keyboard shortcut, Windows + R,

79
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.190
opens a small window on her computer

80
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:10.690
that can run commands directly.

81
00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.630
The second, Ctrl + V,

82
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.650
pastes the hidden command into that window.

83
00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.330
And pressing enter runs it.

84
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.230
In seconds, harmful software installs itself

85
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.210
on Sarah's computer.

86
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.230
No file downloaded, no attachment opened.

87
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.410
She executed the whole attack herself

88
00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.810
by following the instructions.

89
00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.150
How to stay safe from ClickFix tactics.

90
00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.410
Two things to remember.

91
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.950
One, treat unexpected links in emails with caution,

92
00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.410
even if they look routine.

93
00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.650
That's usually where ClickFix begins.

94
00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.770
Two, no legitimate website ever asks you

95
00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.450
to press keyboard shortcuts to verify you're human.

96
00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.130
If prompted to press keys,

97
00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.210
close it immediately,

98
00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.630
then run a security scan or contact your IT team.

99
00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:51.530
Phishing isn't just email anymore.

100
00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:52.990
It's a sticker on a wall,

101
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.570
a link in a routine message,

102
00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.430
a verification step that looks a little too official.

103
00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.670
Before you scan, click paste or approve, pause.

104
00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:03.030
Verify and report, slow down.

105
00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.350
That one habit stops most of these attacks

106
00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.170
before they start.
